Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: at an OnSame end, the prolonged edge's own curve where it lies on
both faces' surfaces past the vertex (mitcad#343).

The case: an external thread's flank rounded with 0.1 along its edge with
the chamfer cone at the thread's start. The rounding starts where the
flank comes out of the root cylinder: an OnSame end (PerformOneCorner),
the cone the opposite face, the root cylinder the face at the end; the
cone's edge with the cylinder (a circle, both surfaces of revolution about
the thread's axis) is prolonged past the vertex up to the rounding's
contact on the cone, and that prolongation crosses the cone's seam.
OCCT intersected the cone and the cylinder for it (ChFi3d_ComputeCurves):
the cone is nearly flat there and the ends' parameters on it lay a period
apart, so the intersection's p-curve ran the long way round the cone, its
3d curve up to 0.1 off the circle (tolerance reached 62); the seam was not
found on it and not cut, and the cone's face lost its seam and its edge
with the cylinder (unorientable).

- ChFi3d_ProlongTangentEdge (the edge's own curve and p-curves continued
  from the vertex up to the contact's end, so far for faces tangent along
  the edge) takes, with Transversal, faces not tangent there whose
  surfaces both hold the curve continued (within 10 tolapp3d at three
  points between the vertex and the contact's end). PerformOneCorner tries
  it after the tangent prolongations and before intersecting the surfaces.
  The prolongation is then exact, its p-curve on the cone crosses the
  seam's line, and the seam is cut there as before.
- There, a prolongation running from the edge's last vertex on a periodic
  curve (a circle) kept its p-curves in the parameters the edge has, while
  the trimmed 3d curve's parameters came into the curve's first period: the
  pieces split at the seam took p-curves a period off on the face beside
  (an unorientable face). The p-curves are parametrized as the 3d curve
  then, as they already were for a prolongation from the first vertex.
- Where the prolongation is still an intersection of the two surfaces (the
  edge's curve an approximation ending at the vertex: the thread modelled
  again, its root a spline), its ends on the periodic opposite face are
  taken in one period (ChFi3d_Recale, as on the face at the end): they lay
  a period apart across the cone's seam and the intersection ran the long
  way round.
- Split at the opposite face's seam, the pieces of such a prolongation
  take the seam's crossing in the curve's own range (on a circle's basis
  the crossing's parameter came in its first period, outside a range
  across the parameters' origin) and their own p-curves, each moved by
  whole periods into the face's domain (the p-curve projected there anew
  lay a period off for the piece from the vertex: a rib's foot on a rod,
  its rounding's end across the rod's seam).

Nothing else changes: where the edge's curve continued leaves either
surface, the surfaces are intersected as before.

Upstream: new.
